 "poem": "XIII. OMNES DE ILLUMINUS domine.\n145    Now come, and now go; for th' midnight,\nWith the pale moon's pale light, shall be probably\nThe going of all these dunces, obliged\nIn time to feed on lies; for again\nI hear thee crying aloud: behold!\nWhat though (and what have I not enough!) then,\nIf thou, as thou art, dost not drink alone,\nOr satiate thyself with more delight,\nYet will it comfort me that alone\nI am a dull sinner, who can't recite\nA word in defence of that monstrous known,",
